# a project requirement. Committed as this artifact's FIRST commit, before anything is hashed.
#
# One paragraph per rule, which is the comment discipline this repository
# inherited from the only .gitattributes analog available. That analog's own
# scope is the argument against it: it was narrow and per-pattern (*.sh plus one
# named path) and its second stanza had to explain that "the *.sh rule above
# does NOT cover it". A broad rule needs no such stanza.

# THE NORMATIVE RULE. Every text blob enters the index as LF regardless of the
# committer's core.autocrlf setting or operating system.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL, and why it is committed FIRST rather than
# alongside everything else. canonkit.py is authored in the tooling repository
# and hashed HERE. A checkout with different end-of-line settings gives a
# different digest for byte-identical content, so the vendoring assertion fails
# for a reason that has nothing to do with the core's content -- and the
# artifact then looks tampered with when it is not.
#
# THE FAILURE ARISES FROM A MISMATCH BETWEEN TWO REPOSITORIES, NOT FROM EITHER
# ONE ALONE. That was measured, in a three-arm experiment, before this template
# existed: normalizing only the source repository does not close it. BOTH ends
# need this rule, which is why the tooling repository carries the identical line
# and why every generated artifact carries this file.
#
# Committing it first is not tidiness either. Git applies these attributes when
# a blob ENTERS the index, so any file committed before this rule is in force
# keeps whatever line endings it was written with -- permanently, until someone
# renormalizes. A commit that added this file alongside the rest of the tree
# would reproduce the exact defect the file exists to prevent, inside the
# artifact meant to demonstrate the fix.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Genuinely binary payloads, marked so git never attempts text conversion on
# them. A normalized binary is a corrupted binary, and the corruption is silent.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
*.gz binary
*.zip binary
*.7z binary
*.parquet binary
*.gguf binary
*.safetensors binary
*.npy binary
*.npz binary
